\subsection{F3--F5: Factorial Ablation (Details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial})}
\label{sec:f3f5_summary}

The factorial ablation establishes that trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure are \emph{jointly necessary} for CIVO. Neither factor alone is sufficient: unexposed trained embedding (008a) maintains NVE $\leq 0.93\times$, frozen embedding with full multi-rate exposure (015b) never exceeds $0.47\times$, and removed embedding (008f) shows NVE $\leq 0.97\times$ (Table~\ref{tab:causal_matrix}, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}). Degradation scales monotonically with $\dt$ ($-47\%$ at $\dt{=}2$, $-81\%$ at $\dt{=}4$), and horizon/subsampling are secondary factors that modulate magnitude without triggering CIVO independently.

